.TH ACTIVATE_LLM 1 "2026-08-10" "ragfarm" "ragfarm LLM life-cycle"
.SH NAME
activate_llm.py \- bind a registered model to a vLLM slot, and manage the GPU memory budget across slots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scripts/activate_llm.py
.RB [ --status ]
.RB [ -s
.IR SLOT ]
.RB [ -m
.IR MODEL " | " -a
.IR ALIAS ]
.RB [ --clear ]
.RB [ --no-restart ]
.PP
Run with no arguments at all for an interactive session: it prints the slot
table, offers activate/clear, and echoes the equivalent command line before
acting.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B activate_llm.py
is the second of the three LLM life-cycle tools.
.BR fetch_llm (1)
puts a model on disk and into the registry;
.B activate_llm.py
decides which registered models are
.I resident in GPU memory
right now; and
.BR active.json (1)
is the file both of them read and write.
.PP
It performs five things in one step, and doing any of them by hand is how
deployments break:
.IP 1. 4
Resolves the model you named against the registry, refusing anything that is
missing from disk or only partially downloaded.
.IP 2. 4
Computes the slot's share of GPU memory and
.B refuses
the change if the total across slots would exceed the ceiling.
.IP 3. 4
Writes
.IR .env.slot<N> ,
the per-slot configuration the systemd unit reads.
.IP 4. 4
Restarts the slot's unit, waiting first if another slot is mid-start.
.IP 5. 4
Re-binds Open WebUI's presets to whatever is now resident.
.SS What a slot is
vLLM serves exactly
.B one
base model per process. There is no multi-model mode: several
.B --served-model-name
values are aliases for the same weights, and LoRA adapters need a shared base.
A "slot" is therefore ragfarm's name for one vLLM instance:
.PP
.RS
.nf
slot 0  ->  systemd ragfarm-vllm@0.service  ->  127.0.0.1:8080/v1
slot 1  ->  systemd ragfarm-vllm@1.service  ->  127.0.0.1:8082/v1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Port is
.IR "8080 + 2*N" .
The stride is 2 because 8081 belongs to the reranker. Two occupied slots mean
two models resident at once, which is what makes switching model mid-conversation
possible in Open WebUI with the context intact.
.PP
There is
.B no per-slot Python environment.
Both slots are served by the single
.I .venv-vllm
interpreter named in the unit template; the project's other
.I .venv
is a separate environment for the ingester, the services and these scripts. See
.BR env (1).
.SH THE MEMORY BUDGET
This is the part you cannot skip, and the reason this script exists rather than
a hand-edited unit file.
.PP
vLLM's
.B --gpu-memory-utilization
is a fraction of
.B total
device memory, and every instance computes it
.B independently
\- neither slot knows the other exists. Two slots each asking for the
single-model default of 0.50 will OOM. On a GB10 there is no separate VRAM to
hide in either: the 121.7 GiB is shared with the embedder, the reranker, Qdrant,
the container plane and the OS.
.PP
So the per-slot fraction is derived, not chosen:
.PP
.RS
.nf
util = (weights + KV_TARGET_GIB + OVERHEAD_GIB) / TOTAL_GIB
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I weights
comes from the size
.BR fetch_llm (1)
recorded
.I after
verifying the download against the Hub \- never from
.BR du ,
because a half-finished checkpoint measures small and would under-allocate the
slot. The sum across all slots must stay under
.BR BUDGET_CEILING " (0.72)."
Exceeding it is a hard refusal, not a warning.
.PP
Constants live at the top of the script:
.TP
.B TOTAL_GIB " (121.7)"
Unified memory as the driver reports it.
.TP
.B KV_TARGET_GIB " (8.0)"
Per-slot KV cache target. Override for one run with the
.B RAGFARM_KV_GIB
environment variable when a slot needs a longer context.
.TP
.B OVERHEAD_GIB " (3.0)"
CUDA context, activations, cudagraph pools.
.TP
.B BUDGET_CEILING " (0.72)"
Leaves at least 28% for everything that is not an LLM.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -s ", " --slot " " \fISLOT\fR
Slot to operate on, 0-based. Required unless
.B --status
is given or the session is interactive.
.TP
.BR -m ", " --model " " \fIMODEL\fR
Select by the registry's
.B model
field, which is the directory name under
.IR models/llm/ ,
e.g.
.IR Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B-Instruct-NVFP4 .
Matched exactly.
.TP
.BR -a ", " --alias " " \fIALIAS\fR
Select by the registry's
.B alias
field \- the name vLLM advertises on
.I /v1/models
and the name Open WebUI binds its preset to.
.TP
.B --clear
Free the slot: unbind it in the registry, delete its
.IR .env.slot<N> ,
stop its unit, drop trailing empty slots from
.BR active[] ,
and re-bind the Open WebUI presets. This is how you get down to a single
resident model. See
.B SINGLE LARGE MODEL
below.
.TP
.B --status
Print the slot table and the total budget, and exit. Changes nothing. Safe at
any time.
.TP
.B --no-restart
Write the configuration but leave systemd alone. For staging a change you will
apply later, or for editing the registry on a machine that is not running the
services. The slot keeps serving the
.B old
model until something restarts it.
.SH SINGLE LARGE MODEL
The default deployment runs two slots because differential diagnosis \- the same
conversation answered by two models \- is worth the memory. A 70B-class model
does not fit alongside anything: at FP8 its weights alone are around 67 GiB, so
with KV and overhead it needs roughly 0.64 of the budget against a 0.72 ceiling.
.PP
Free the other slot first. The order matters: if you try to activate the large
model while slot 1 is still bound, the budget check refuses and nothing changes
\- which is the correct behaviour, not a bug to work around.
.PP
.RS
.nf
# 1. See what is resident and how much of the budget is spoken for.
scripts/activate_llm.py --status

# 2. Free slot 1. Stops the unit, deletes .env.slot1, re-binds the OWUI
#    presets so the picker stops offering a model that is no longer served.
scripts/activate_llm.py -s 1 --clear

# 3. Confirm the budget actually came back before committing to the big model.
scripts/activate_llm.py --status

# 4. Bind the large model to slot 0.
scripts/activate_llm.py -s 0 -m <directory-name>
.fi
.RE
.PP
To go back to a pair, activate slot 0 with something smaller
.B first,
then slot 1 \- one at a time, waiting for each to answer
.I /v1/models
before starting the next. The script enforces this itself when it can see
another slot mid-start, but it cannot see a unit you started by hand.
.SH WHY SLOTS START SEQUENTIALLY
vLLM profiles free GPU memory during engine initialisation. If a second instance
is allocating at the same moment, the first sees free memory move underneath it
and dies with one of:
.PP
.RS
.nf
AssertionError: Error in memory profiling. Initial free memory X, current Y
ValueError: No available memory for the cache blocks
.fi
.RE
.PP
Neither message points at the race, and both look like a wrong budget. Observed
for real on 2026-08-05. The script waits for any slot in
.B activating
state before restarting another, for up to 30 minutes \- a cold FlashInfer JIT
build is genuinely that slow the first time a checkpoint is served.
.SH WHY OPEN WEBUI IS RE-BOUND
Open WebUI presets carry the system prompt, the tool list and the sampler
settings, and they bind to a model
.B by alias.
Change what a slot serves without re-running the preset setup and the preset
still names an alias that no longer exists. The model picker then offers only
the
.B raw
base models \- no system prompt, no tools, no grounding rules. Nothing looks
broken; answers are just silently ungrounded.
.PP
This happened on 2026-08-07 after switching both slots from NVFP4 to FP8, which
is why the re-bind is automatic and runs on
.B --clear
as well as on activation. If it fails, the script says so and prints the command
to run by hand; do not ignore that warning.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Everyday: what is running?
.RS
.nf
scripts/activate_llm.py --status
.fi
.RE
.SS Swap the model in slot 0
.RS
.nf
scripts/activate_llm.py -s 0 -m Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B-Thinking-FP8
.fi
.RE
.SS Give a slot a bigger KV cache for one activation
.RS
.nf
RAGFARM_KV_GIB=16 scripts/activate_llm.py -s 0 -m Qwen3-VL-8B-Thinking
.fi
.RE
.SS Full scenario: take delivery of two 70B-class vision models and run one
Everything below uses only the project's own tools. No ad-hoc
.BR curl ,
no hand-edited unit files, no
.B systemctl
except through the scripts.
.PP
.RS
.nf
# --- 0. Where are we starting from? ------------------------------------
# Slot table, per-slot memory share, and the total against the ceiling.
scripts/activate_llm.py --status

# Are the units healthy, and what is each actually serving? --status reads
# the registry, which is our INTENT; this reads the running system.
systemctl --no-pager --plain status 'ragfarm-vllm@*.service'
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/models
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8082/v1/models

# --- 1. Take delivery of the models ------------------------------------
# fetch_llm.py downloads, verifies every file against the Hub, records the
# verified size, and only then appends to the registry. Names must be unique
# or it refuses BEFORE spending hours on the transfer -- so pass them.
scripts/fetch_llm.py -m RedHatAI/Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct-FP8-dynamic \\
    --alias qwen25-vl-72b-instruct-fp8 \\
    --preset ragfarm-vision-72b \\
    --display 'Qwen2.5-VL 72B Instruct FP8 (dense)' \\
    --profile vision-instruct \\
    --comment 'dense 72B; fits alone only -- clear the other slot first'

scripts/fetch_llm.py -m RedHatAI/Llama-3.2-90B-Vision-Instruct-FP8-dynamic \\
    --alias llama32-90b-vision-fp8 \\
    --preset ragfarm-vision-llama90b \\
    --display 'Llama 3.2 90B Vision Instruct FP8' \\
    --profile vision-instruct \\
    --comment 'dense 90B; exceeds BUDGET_CEILING even alone -- see man page'

# Confirm both landed complete. --verify re-checks disk against the Hub and
# changes nothing; it is the honest answer to "did that download finish?".
scripts/fetch_llm.py --verify

# --- 2. Make room ------------------------------------------------------
# A 70B-class model cannot share the box. Free slot 1 first: this stops the
# unit, deletes .env.slot1 and re-binds the OWUI presets.
scripts/activate_llm.py -s 1 --clear

# Prove the budget came back before committing to the big model.
scripts/activate_llm.py --status

# --- 3. Activate the one model we want ---------------------------------
scripts/activate_llm.py -s 0 -m Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct-FP8-dynamic

# --- 4. Verify it is really up -----------------------------------------
# First start on a new checkpoint is slow: FlashInfer compiles kernels.
journalctl -u ragfarm-vllm@0.service -f

# The service being 'active' is not proof it can serve; ask it.
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/models
scripts/activate_llm.py --status
.fi
.RE
.PP
The Llama entry in that scenario is deliberate. It downloads and registers
fine, and then
.B activate_llm.py
refuses it, because at roughly 84 GiB of weights it needs about 0.78 of the
budget against a 0.72 ceiling \- with nothing else resident at all. That refusal
is the guard working. To run it anyway you would have to lower
.BR KV_TARGET_GIB ,
raise
.BR BUDGET_CEILING ,
and accept that you are eating the headroom the embedder, reranker and Qdrant
are living in. Measure before you do.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I models/llm/active.json
The registry. Source of truth for what exists and what is bound. See
.BR active.json (1).
.TP
.I .env.slot<N>
Generated per slot; the unit reads it. Never edit by hand \- it is rewritten on
every activation and deleted on
.BR --clear .
.TP
.I .env
Global settings shared by both slots. See
.BR env (1).
.TP
.I manifests/ragfarm-vllm@.service
The templated systemd unit. Install with
.B sudo cp
into
.I /etc/systemd/system/
followed by
.BR "sudo systemctl daemon-reload" .
.TP
.I infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
Re-run automatically after every activation and clear.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RAGFARM_KV_GIB
Overrides the per-slot KV cache target for this invocation only. It changes the
computed
.B util
written into
.IR .env.slot<N> ,
so it persists until the next activation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or
.B --status
printed.
.TP
.B 1
Refused: budget exceeded, checkpoint missing or partial, or an inconsistent
registry. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fetch_llm (1),
.BR active.json (1),
.BR env (1)
.PP
.I docs/decisions/ADR-0013
for the engine split and the slots design;
.I docs/deployment.md
for the running system as a whole.
.SH NOTES
Run as
.BR dave ,
never as root. The script calls
.B sudo systemctl
only for the specific unit operations it needs.
